Here’s a quick Java snippet that shows how you’d prepare the `CallableStatement` on your `ProxyConnection` instance (`proxyConn`) with the exact flags you requested:

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class SalesProcedureDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Assume proxyConn is already instantiated and connected
        ProxyConnection proxyConn = /* obtain your proxy connection here */;

        // Prepare the CALL statement with the desired result‑set characteristics
        CallableStatement cs = proxyConn.prepareCall(
                "CALL totalSales(?)",
                ResultSet.TYPE_SCROLL_INSENSITIVE,   // scrollable but not sensitive to changes
                ResultSet.CONCUR_READ_ONLY,          // read‑only cursor
                ResultSet.CLOSE_CURSORS_AT_COMMIT);  // close cursors when transaction commits

        // Bind input parameters (if any)
        // e.g., cs.setInt(1, someValue);

        // Execute the stored procedure
        boolean hasResults = cs.execute();

        // Process results if the procedure returns a result set
        while (hasResults) {
            try (ResultSet rs = cs.getResultSet()) {
                while (rs.next()) {
                    // Example: retrieve a column named "total"
                    double total = rs.getDouble("total");
                    System.out.println("Total sales: " + total);
                }
            }
            hasResults = cs.getMoreResults();
        }

        // Clean up
        cs.close();
    }
}
```

### What this does

| Parameter | Meaning |
|-----------|---------|
| `"CALL totalSales(?)"` | The SQL string that calls the stored procedure. Replace `?` with actual bind variables as needed. |
| `ResultSet.TYPE_SCROLL_INSENSITIVE` | Allows scrolling forward/backward through the result set, but the data won’t reflect changes made after the cursor was opened. |
| `ResultSet.CONCUR_READ_ONLY` | The cursor is read‑only – you cannot update rows via this result set. |
| `ResultSet.CLOSE_CURSORS_AT_COMMIT` | Cursors will automatically close when the current transaction commits. |

Feel free to adjust the parameter binding section (`cs.set…`) based on the actual inputs your procedure expects. If the procedure doesn’t return a result set, you can skip the `getResultSet()` handling and just check the update count instead.
